WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
This green moss cushion is haploid, like a human egg.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.460
So where is the diploid moss?

3
00:00:10.583 --> 00:00:13.463
A haploid cell carries one set of chromosomes.

4
00:00:14.843 --> 00:00:16.963
We write that as n, and colour it gold.

5
00:00:18.243 --> 00:00:22.223
A diploid cell carries two sets, written 2n, and coloured teal.

6
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:27.257
In a normal life cycle, only two kinds of event change that count.

7
00:00:28.817 --> 00:00:29.837
Meiosis halves it.

8
00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.637
Every fusion of nuclei adds the sets together.

9
00:00:35.077 --> 00:00:38.017
Mitosis, which copies a cell, never changes the count.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.480
Now draw a land plant's life as a wheel.

11
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.000
A diploid body, the sporophyte, or spore plant, makes spores by meiosis.

12
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.880
Each spore is a single cell.

13
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:56.600
A spore divides by mitosis into a second body, the gametophyte, or gamete plant.

14
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.600
It is already haploid, so it makes eggs and sperm by mitosis.

15
00:01:02.783 --> 00:01:04.383
Then sperm and egg fuse.

16
00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:09.063
The zygote, the single fertilized cell, has two sets again.

17
00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.343
It divides by mitosis into a new sporophyte.

18
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:15.773
So the wheel has two arcs.

19
00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:19.973
From meiosis to fertilization, the path is gold.

20
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:24.333
From fertilization to the next meiosis, it is teal.

21
00:01:25.653 --> 00:01:29.013
Other fusions can make other labels, as the next film shows.

22
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:36.707
Now the moss. The gold arc swells into the leafy green shoot, so that shoot is the

23
00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:41.947
gametophyte. A sperm swims through a film of rain or dew to an egg on it.

24
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:45.727
The teal arc shrinks to a thin stalk and a capsule.

25
00:01:46.927 --> 00:01:48.267
That is the diploid moss.

26
00:01:49.687 --> 00:01:51.467
Meiosis in the capsule makes the spores.

27
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:56.337
Now a fern. Here the arcs swap sizes.

28
00:01:57.657 --> 00:01:59.617
The big frond is the sporophyte, teal.

29
00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:03.157
Spore cases under it make spores by meiosis.

30
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.270
Each spore grows into a small plant on the ground.

31
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.130
Gold or teal: the spore, and that little plant?

32
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:23.497
Both gold. They come after the meiosis gate, and no fusion has happened yet.

33
00:02:24.757 --> 00:02:29.297
The little plant is a prothallus, a flat, often heart-shaped gametophyte.

34
00:02:30.357 --> 00:02:31.457
It lives on its own.

35
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.010
Now compare your own wheel.

36
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.370
In humans, meiosis makes eggs and sperm directly.

37
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.790
The gold arc shrinks to the gametes alone.

38
00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.430
There is no haploid body.

39
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.020
In the green algae closest to land plants, the teal arc shrinks instead.

40
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.560
The zygote is often the only diploid cell.

41
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.080
It undergoes meiosis when it germinates.

42
00:02:58.083 --> 00:02:59.623
So size tells you nothing.

43
00:03:00.943 --> 00:03:02.943
The big body can be gold or teal.

44
00:03:04.143 --> 00:03:07.443
Find meiosis and fusion first, and every label follows.

45
00:03:08.617 --> 00:03:10.077
One problem remains.

46
00:03:10.077 --> 00:03:15.177
In both the moss and the fern, sperm swim to the egg through water.

47
00:03:16.077 --> 00:03:17.917
What can a plant do where there is none?
